u \in \mathcal{S}} \mathfrak{g}^
u$ and whose reductive part equals $\mathfrak{s}$?". Our main results is that, for a classical simple Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ and a saturated $\mathcal{S} \subset \mathcal{R}$, the condition $(\operatorname{Sym}^\cdot(\mathcal{M}))^{\mathfrak{s}} = \mathbf{C}$ is necessary and sufficient for the existence of such a $\mathfrak{p}$. In contrast, we show that this statement is no longer true for the exceptional Lie algebras $\mathrm{F}_4, \mathrm{E}_6, \mathrm{E}_7$, and $\mathrm{E}_8$. Finally, we discuss the problem in the case when $\mathcal{S}$ is not saturated.

